# SENTINEL_PRIME - version control policy
#
# This file exists to make one thing explicit to whoever (or whatever) reads
# the repository next: NOTHING IS EXCLUDED ON PURPOSE.
#
# There was a real loss in this project. On 2026-10-06 a single append destroyed
# 27 recorded corrections (M-01..M-27) from
# 11_BRAIN/04_METHOD/MISSTEPS_AND_CORRECTIONS.md, and the record of the loss
# said - correctly, at the time - that the content was unrecoverable.
#
# Twenty-three of them were in fact still on disk in a reference snapshot, and
# were restored on 2026-10-10. The other four never were.
#
# A `.gitignore` is where content goes to die quietly. Nothing here is listed,
# so nothing is hidden. If something here is too large for GitHub, that is a
# decision to be made out loud, by a person, in a commit message - not a line
# added to this file at 2am.
#
# Only genuine operating-system noise is excluded, and none of it is project
# material.

# OS / editor scratch
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.DS_Store
*.swp
*~

# Python bytecode - regenerated output, in the same class as the editor scratch
# above, not project content.
#
# Added 2026-10-28. `__pycache__/*.pyc` is machine- and version-specific
# bytecode that Python rebuilds on the next run from the source that IS tracked.
# Committing it puts a different binary in every clone, creates merge conflicts
# on a file that carries no information, and buries real changes.
#
# Nothing is lost by excluding it: the source file is the artefact, and the
# bytecode is derived from it. Same rule as `.github-repo/` below - the
# exclusion is stated out loud, in the file and in the commit message.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# The working clone of this same repository.
#
# This is the one DIRECTORY exclusion added on 2026-10-28, and it is stated out
# loud here and in the commit message, because the rule above is "nothing is
# excluded on purpose". The exception is narrow and is not project material:
#
#   .github-repo/ is a git clone of THIS repository, placed inside it by the
#   operator's instruction on 2026-10-28. It holds a second copy of every
#   tracked file. Without this line, `git add -A` would try to add a nested
#   repository's entire contents to this one, and git would record it as a
#   gitlink - a 1400-file commit that says nothing.
#
# Nothing that exists ONLY in .github-repo/ is lost by excluding it: it is a
# clone, and everything in it is in the history this repository already has.
.github-repo/

# Machine-generated backup artefacts - the two directories the backup system
# writes and rotates on its own.
#
# Added 2026-10-28. `08_SELF_IMPROVEMENT/backups/` and
# `08_SELF_IMPROVEMENT/backup_metadata/` are written by `backup_system.py` and
# `self_modify_engine.py`, which keep the last 10 copies per file and delete the
# older ones (`_rotate_backups`, `max_keep=10`). 32 of these files were tracked,
# so every runtime commit carried 32 machine-written files, and the ones the
# rotation removed appeared as DELETIONS in the audit trail - noise that reads
# like data loss and is not.
#
# Nothing is lost by excluding them. The rotation is the mechanism: it keeps the
# newest 10 on disk, and every earlier copy is still in this repository's
# history, so any backup can be brought back with `git show <commit>:<path>`.
#
# The deliberate safety copies made by hand are NOT excluded. The project
# convention is that every edited file gets a `.bak` next to it, and those
# copies stay tracked. Only the two machine-written directories are excluded -
# never a blanket `*.bak`, which would silently disable the convention.
08_SELF_IMPROVEMENT/backups/
08_SELF_IMPROVEMENT/backup_metadata/
